Tree – The main class representing an entire tree. It creates and
organizes the trunk, leaves, and fruits at a given position.
Trunk – Represents the tree’s trunk. It is a static object that does
not respond to interactions but serves as the physical and visual base of the tree.
Leaf – Represents a tree leaf. It animates (oscillates in size and rotation) and
reacts to the avatar’s jumps.
Fruit – Represents a fruit on the tree. When the avatar touches it, it disappears
temporarily, restores energy to the avatar, and reappears later.
Flora – Responsible for generating multiple trees over a specified range. It uses
Tree objects to populate the environment.
JumpObserver – An interface implemented by any object that wants to respond to the
avatar’s jump (e.g., leaves, fruits).
The Tree class creates and manages instances of Trunk, Leaf, and Fruit.
Both Leaf and Fruit implement the JumpObserver interface to allow them to react when
 the avatar jumps.
The Flora class uses a ground height function to determine where to place trees
and invokes Tree to create them.
The avatar registers JumpObserver instances using a callback, allowing all leaves
and fruits to receive updates on jumps.
Yes, the implementation uses several design principles and patterns:
Observer Pattern – Implemented via the JumpObserver interface. The avatar acts as a
subject that notifies registered observers (e.g., leaves, fruits) on jump events.
Factory-Like Composition – Tree acts as a factory/composer, creating and assembling
parts (trunk, leaves, fruits) into a single tree structure.
Dependency Injection – Flora receives behavior (e.g., how to add energy or handle jumps)
via consumers and functions passed in its constructor, promoting flexibility and separation of concerns.